Initial attempt was brute force where you first compare using the first index. 
It would then loop in a second loop to see if the value from the subtraction existed and return the index if it did.
This was slow as you would have to reapetedly check through the whole array if the value existed and if it didn't the process would just restart all over again.
I then though what if I sorted it and then used a two pointer approach where I compared the sum rather than the value from subtraction.
This worked better as I would only need to loop through the array once and even shorter as it was from both ends.
An even faster way however was the use of hashamps where using the same logic as the first attempt, instead of looping to find the right value you used a hashmap to immediately search fr the value without having to go through it all.

